<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.10.2: Expanding from Factored Form to Standard Form</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02499</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.10.2: Expanding from Factored Form to Standard Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6aba5080-5aea-4227-a03a-36af5499e384</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Recall learning how to identify and factor perfect square trinomials in Unit 6. Here are some perfect squares in factored and standard forms, and an expression showing how the two forms are related.</para>
<para>Study the first few examples in rows 1 and 2. Create a similar table to the one modeled below. Copy the table (or use a copy provided by your teacher) and then complete the missing numbers in rows 3 – 6 in the rest of the table.</para>
<table class="os-raise-wideadjustedtable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Row</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Factored Form</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Standard Form</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>1</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((x+4)^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((1x)^2+2(1x)(4)+4^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x^2+8x+16\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>2</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((2x+5)^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((2x)^2+2(2x)(5)+5^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(4x^2+20x+25\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>3</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((3x-4)^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((3x)^2+2(\_\_\_\;x)(\_\_\_)+(\_\_\_)^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(9x^2−24x+16\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>4</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((5x + \_\_\_)^2\) </para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((\_\_\_\;x)^2+2(\_\_\_\;x)(\_\_\_)+(\_\_\_)^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(25x^2+30x + \_\_\_\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>5</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((kx+m)^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((\_\_\_\;x)^2+2(\_\_\_\;x)(\_\_\_)+(\_\_\_)^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para> \((\_\_\_)^2x^2+ \_\_\_\;x+ (\_\_\_)^2\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Enter the missing values from row 3.</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-wideadjustedtable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Row</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Factored Form</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Standard Form</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>3</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((3x-4)^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\((3x)^2+2(\_\_\_\;x)(\_\_\_)+(\_\_\_)^2\)
          \((3x)^2+2(\;\underline{\;\mathbf a\boldsymbol.\;}\;x)(\underline{\;\mathbf b\boldsymbol.\;})+(\underline{\;\mathbf c\boldsymbol.\;})^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(9x^2−24x+16\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
